OAI_PAPER({"slug": "QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026", "title": "QMA-hardness of continuum Coulomb energy with unit nuclear charges", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026/QMA-hardness-of-continuum-Coulomb-energy-with-unit-nuclear-charges-September-24-2026.pdf", "family": "275", "family_title": "QMA-hardness of continuum Coulomb energy", "abstract_html": "We prove that approximating the electronic ground-energy infimum for clamped unit-charge nuclei is QMA-hard on the full spinful fermionic continuum space. A deterministic classical polynomial-time reduction produces polynomially many nuclei at distinct rational positions and polynomially many electrons, with polynomial rational bit lengths and threshold separation at least one. No orbital basis, magnetic field, or additional external potential is supplied, and no binding assumption is imposed.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Unit-charge Coulomb hardness (paper's Theorem 1).}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Unit-charge Coulomb hardness (paper's Theorem 1).}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M\\ge 1\\) clamped nuclei have pairwise distinct positions \\(R_1,\\ldots,R_M\\in\\mathbb Q^3\\), with nuclear charges \\(Z_\\alpha=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(N\\ge 1\\) be the electron number, encoded in unary.</p><p class=\"li\">In atomic units, define the electronic Hamiltonian\n  \\[\n  H=-\\frac12\\sum_{i=1}^N\\Delta_{x_i}\n  -\\sum_{i=1}^N\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^M\\frac1{|x_i-R_\\alpha|}\n  +\\sum_{1\\le i&lt;j\\le N}\\frac1{|x_i-x_j|}. \\tag{H}\n  \\]\n  Clamped nuclear repulsion is omitted.</p><p class=\"li\">The Hilbert space and form domain are\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal H_N=\\bigwedge^N\\bigl(L^2(\\mathbb R^3)\\otimes\\mathbb C^2\\bigr),\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal Q_N=\n  H^1\\bigl(\\mathbb R^{3N};(\\mathbb C^2)^{\\otimes N}\\bigr)\\cap\\mathcal H_N.\n  \\]\n  Antisymmetry exchanges position and spin together; no spin sector is selected.</p><p class=\"li\">The operator is the lower-bounded self-adjoint operator associated with the closed Coulomb quadratic form on \\(\\mathcal Q_N\\). Write\n  \\[\n  E_0=\\inf\\operatorname{spec}H,\n  \\]\n  equivalently the infimum of the form over normalized vectors in \\(\\mathcal Q_N\\), whether or not the infimum is attained.</p><p class=\"li\">Nuclear coordinates and thresholds \\(a&lt;b\\) are rational numbers, encoded by signed binary numerators and positive binary denominators in lowest terms, using a fixed self-delimiting encoding. Let \\(L\\) be the total binary and unary input length.</p><p class=\"li\">The promise is\n  \\[\n  b-a\\ge L^{-1},\n  \\]\n  with YES instances satisfying \\(E_0\\le a\\) and NO instances satisfying \\(E_0\\ge b\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>This electronic promise problem is QMA-hard under deterministic classical polynomial-time many-one reductions. The reduction produces polynomially many nuclei and electrons, polynomial rational bit lengths, and threshold separation at least one. Its only output data are the nuclear positions, electron number, and thresholds.</p><p>Thus the bounded-charge problem holds with the absolute charge bound \\(Z_{\\max}=1\\) and precision exponent \\(c=1\\). No neutrality, binding, existence of a ground eigenvector, molecular spectral-gap, or lower nuclear-separation promise is imposed. No orbital basis, electronic state, magnetic field, or additional external potential is supplied. Membership in QMA is not asserted. The threshold separation is an input precision promise, not an excitation-gap assumption.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 1 provides a QMA-hard signed rational Heisenberg source with polynomial geometry and inverse-polynomial decision gap. Proposition 2 converts it to a positive-exchange spin model, with explicit scalar shift and controlled error. Proposition 3 realizes its bottom energy, up to scale and scalar, in the calibrated finite fermion Hamiltonian while retaining direct intersite Coulomb terms and their virtual-excitation denominators.</p><p>Proposition 4 synthesizes the required one-particle wells and counterterm from a nonnegative continuous nuclear density, establishes a fixed complementary gap, and computes the localized-mode compression. Proposition 5 replaces that density by distinct rationally positioned unit nuclei, with separate arbitrary-state form and localized compression estimates.</p><p>Proposition 6 gives a lower bound on every antisymmetric continuum form-domain state, uniformly in spin, by eliminating the complement of the mode space through square completion. Together with the compressed Hamiltonian identification, Proposition 7 compares the full continuum infimum with the finite fermion energy.</p><p>Proposition 8 supplies the explicit affine physical-energy map and rational thresholds preserving YES and NO instances, with separation greater than one. Proposition 9 shows that all output data are computable by a deterministic classical polynomial-time procedure, with polynomially many unit nuclei and electrons and polynomial rational bit lengths.</p><p>The output contains only nuclear positions, electron number, and thresholds. Its separation \\(b-a\\ge1\\) implies \\(b-a\\ge L^{-1}\\). The minimization is over the full spinful fermionic continuum space, without a supplied orbital basis, magnetic field, additional external potential, selected spin sector, or binding assumption. With electronic energy defined by omitting clamped nuclear repulsion, this proves Theorem 1.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{The lattice source and rational normalization.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{The lattice source and rational normalization.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Start with the field-free square-lattice Heisenberg energy problem from Cubitt, Montanaro, and Piddock [CMP19, Theorem 48 and Section 10.1], using the polynomial-weight spatially sparse construction of their Lemma 47.</p><p class=\"li\">The Hamiltonian on a finite, nonwrapping square-lattice subgraph is\n  \\[\n  H_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\sum_e J_e h^s_{LR},\n  \\qquad\n  h^s_{LR}=\\sigma_L\\cdot\\sigma_R,\n  \\]\n  where the components of \\(\\sigma\\) are Pauli matrices and there are no one-site terms.</p><p class=\"li\">The cited construction supplies polynomial bounds on lattice size and coefficient magnitudes and an inverse-polynomial promise gap.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a deterministic polynomial-time normalization producing signed rational coefficients and rational thresholds \\(a_s,b_s\\), preserving both promise implications, such that the graph has polynomial geometric extent and\n\\[\nb_s-a_s&gt;0\n\\]\nis inverse polynomial. Set\n\\[\nn=\\max(2,\\text{source input size}),\n\\qquad\n\\gamma=\\min(1,b_s-a_s).\n\\]\nAll subsequent polynomial choices may use fixed size, magnitude, and inverse-gap bounds for this normalized source. Zero weights may be omitted, and the vertex set may be assumed nonempty.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\delta&gt;0\\) be a known rational inverse-polynomial lower bound for the incoming promise gap, and let \\(q\\) be the number of weighted edges. Set\n\\[\n\\epsilon_0=\\delta/100.\n\\]\nRounding each weight within \\(\\epsilon_0/\\max(1,q)\\) changes the Hamiltonian norm by at most \\(3\\epsilon_0\\), since \\(\\|h^s_{ij}\\|=3\\).</p><p>Write the incoming scalar as \\(c_0I\\), with \\(c_0=0\\) if absent, and the incoming thresholds as \\(a_0,b_0\\). Approximate these three numbers within \\(\\epsilon_0\\), obtaining \\(\\widetilde c_0,\\widetilde a_0,\\widetilde b_0\\). The rational Hamiltonian without the scalar has valid thresholds\n\\[\na_s=\\widetilde a_0-\\widetilde c_0+5\\epsilon_0,\n\\qquad\nb_s=\\widetilde b_0-\\widetilde c_0-5\\epsilon_0.\n\\]\nThe offsets allow \\(3\\epsilon_0\\) for the Hamiltonian, \\(\\epsilon_0\\) for the scalar, and \\(\\epsilon_0\\) for the threshold. Thus both promise implications survive, and\n\\[\nb_s-a_s\\ge\\delta-12\\epsilon_0&gt;0.\n\\]\nPolynomial magnitudes and inverse-polynomial accuracy require only polynomial rational bit lengths.</p><p>A connected square-grid component with \\(V\\) vertices has coordinate diameter at most \\(V-1\\), by following lattice paths. Translating components into disjoint strips therefore gives polynomial extent without changing their weighted graphs. Translate coordinates to keep them polynomially bounded, omit zero weights, and add a free spin if necessary to obtain a nonempty vertex set.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Positive spin model with independently adjustable contact lengths.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Positive spin model with independently adjustable contact lengths.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H_{\\mathrm{in}},n,\\gamma\\) be the normalized signed rational Heisenberg instance of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(E(A)\\) for the smallest eigenvalue of a finite-dimensional self-adjoint operator \\(A\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One can construct a positive-exchange model\n\\[\nH_+=\\sum_e K_eh^s_{ij}\n\\]\non \\(m=\\operatorname{poly}(n)\\) sites, with every actual \\(K_e&gt;0\\), and with \\(K_e\\) and \\(K_e^{-1}\\) polynomially bounded. There is a sum of scalar shifts \\(C_s\\) such that\n\\[\n\\left|E(H_+)-E(H_{\\mathrm{in}})+C_s\\right|\n\\le \\frac{4\\gamma}{100}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\nThe intermediate weights and shifts are polynomial-time approximable to inverse-polynomial error.</p><p>The resulting graph has a planar contact layout of polynomial extent with all link lengths initially one and all nonlink distances at least \\(1.4\\). There is a fixed \\(\\epsilon\\in(0,1/100)\\) such that arbitrary prescribed link lengths in \\([1-\\epsilon,1+\\epsilon]\\) can be implemented independently, with original vertices fixed and nonlink distances remaining greater than \\(1.2\\). Positions can be computed to any inverse-polynomial error in polynomial time.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.2, using Lemma 2.1, gives the positive-exchange energy reduction and scalar shift (1). Lemma 2.3 supplies the planar layout and independently adjustable contact lengths for its final links.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Second-order bottom-energy estimate (paper's Lemma 2).}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Second-order bottom-energy estimate (paper's Lemma 2).}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A,B\\) be self-adjoint operators on a finite-dimensional Hilbert space.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(A\\) has kernel projection \\(P\\ne0\\), and set \\(Q=I-P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n  \\[\n  A\\ge gQ,\\qquad g&gt;0,\\qquad \\|B\\|\\le g/4.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The inverse \\(A^{-1}\\) is defined to vanish on \\(\\ker A\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nE(A+B)=\nE\\!\\left(\\left.(PBP-PBA^{-1}BP)\\right|_P\\right)\n+O(\\|B\\|^3/g^2).\n\\]\nThe statement includes \\(Q=0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Indeed,\n\\[\n|E(A+B)|\\le\\|B\\|.\n\\]\nAt that energy the \\(Q\\)-equation can be solved in terms of the nonzero \\(P\\)-component of a ground vector. The inverse on \\(Q\\) used there is\n\\[\n\\left(\\left.A\\right|_Q+\\left.QBQ\\right|_Q-E(A+B)\\right)^{-1};\n\\]\nit differs from \\(\\left.A^{-1}\\right|_Q\\) by \\(O(\\|B\\|/g^2)\\) by the resolvent identity. The \\(P\\)-equation gives the lower estimate.</p><p>For the upper estimate take a unit ground vector \\(p\\) for the displayed compression and use\n\\[\np-A^{-1}Bp\n\\]\nas trial vector. Its energy numerator is the compressed energy plus \\(O(\\|B\\|^3/g^2)\\), and the normalization changes this result by at most the same order.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Sign conversion and subdivision by singlet pairs.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Sign conversion and subdivision by singlet pairs.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In the setting of Proposition 2, first delete source weights below a fixed inverse-polynomial absolute cutoff chosen so that the deletion error is at most \\(\\gamma/100\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a replaced edge \\(e=LR\\) of weight \\(J\\ne0\\), insert a fresh pair \\(A_e,B_e\\), a central bond \\(\\Delta h^s_{A_eB_e}\\), and spokes\n  \\[\n  \\sqrt{\\Delta}\\,v_e\n  \\bigl(h^s_{LA_e}+h^s_{C_eR}\\bigr),\n  \\qquad\n  v_e=\\sqrt{2|J|},\n  \\qquad\n  C_e=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  B_e,&amp;J&gt;0,\\\\\n  A_e,&amp;J&lt;0.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]\n  Here \\(\\Delta\\) is a common large positive integer for the operation.</p><p class=\"li\">Edges may be replaced simultaneously, keeping all unreplaced terms unchanged.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The replacement changes the bottom energy by\n\\[\n-\\sum_e\\left(3\\Delta+\\frac32v_e^2\\right)\n\\]\nup to error at most \\(\\gamma/100\\), for polynomially large \\(\\Delta\\).</p><p>Apply this operation first to all surviving signed edges. Subdivide both resulting spokes twice, using the positive-weight version of the same operation, while never subdividing the original central-pair bond. The final model is positive and has, per surviving input edge, one central bond and two nine-link paths. Its energy obeys (1).</p>", "proof": "<p>Recall that\n\\[\nh^s_{ij}=2\\operatorname{SWAP}_{ij}-I\n\\]\nhas eigenvalues \\(-3,1\\) on the singlet and triplets, respectively. Penalize excitations of the fresh pairs by\n\\[\nA=\\Delta\\sum_e(h^s_{A_eB_e}+3I),\n\\]\nwhose kernel is the tensor product of the pair singlets and whose gap is \\(4\\Delta\\). The remaining operator \\(B\\) has norm at most\n\\[\n\\operatorname{poly}(n)(1+\\sqrt{\\Delta})\n\\]\nwhen the entering model has polynomial size and weights. Its first-order compression is the unreplaced part.</p><p>Only two actions on the same fresh pair contribute to the inverse term, with denominator \\(4\\Delta\\). Pauli products on one member of that singlet have expectation \\(\\delta_{\\mu\\nu}\\), and products across the pair have expectation \\(-\\delta_{\\mu\\nu}\\). Thus the negative inverse term gives\n\\[\nJh^s_{LR}-\\frac32v_e^2I\n\\]\nper edge. Lemma 2.1 permits a polynomially large \\(\\Delta\\) making the error at most \\(\\gamma/100\\).</p><p>For subdivision, one replacement of a positive edge gives a path of length three. In the first subdivision stage replace both spokes. In the second replace all edges of their resulting paths, not the original central-pair bond. Each stage may use its own polynomial scale, and there are only three stages total. Every final link weight and its reciprocal remain polynomially bounded.</p><p>The deletion error and three stage errors total at most \\(4\\gamma/100\\). Summing the scalar shifts gives \\(C_s\\) and (1). All weights and shifts are polynomial-time approximable to inverse-polynomial error; the square roots need not be exact rational outputs.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Planar contact templates and independent length adjustment.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Planar contact templates and independent length adjustment.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the graph produced in Lemma 2.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Start with original grid spacing \\(17\\), orienting edges in the two positive axial directions.</p><p class=\"li\">Within each original-edge gadget, use coordinates along its direction and a perpendicular direction, with \\(L=(0,0)\\) and \\(R=(17,0)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For positive input sign set\n  \\[\n  A_e=(8,0),\\qquad B_e=(9,0).\n  \\]\n  For negative input sign set\n  \\[\n  A_e=(8.5,0),\\qquad B_e=(8.5,1).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The two nine-link paths can be placed so that every link has length one and every nonlink distance is at least \\(1.4\\). A fixed \\(\\epsilon\\in(0,1/100)\\) allows independent prescription of all link lengths in \\([1-\\epsilon,1+\\epsilon]\\), preserving fixed original vertices and nonlink distances greater than \\(1.2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For positive sign the paths join \\(L\\) to \\(A_e\\) and \\(B_e\\) to \\(R\\); for negative sign both paths meet \\(A_e\\). Each path joins two on-axis points \\(8\\) or \\(8.5\\) apart. Orient it in increasing first coordinate. Make the first two, middle fifth, and last two steps unit forward steps. Use two unit forward-sloping steps, numbered \\(3,4\\), with first component \\(3/4\\) for span \\(8\\), or \\(7/8\\) for span \\(8.5\\), balanced by steps \\(6,7\\) with transverse components negated.</p><p>The link lengths equal one. Consecutive separations projected along a main path or backbone are at least \\(3/4\\); nonconsecutive sites on it are separated by at least \\(1.5\\). The extra side site for a negative sign is at distance at least \\(\\sqrt2\\) from sites other than its central neighbor, because the steps immediately there are straight. These bounds also give separation along each entire axial lattice line across successive gadgets.</p><p>Parallel lines cannot interfere. Transverse offsets are at most\n\\[\n2\\sqrt{1-(3/4)^2}&lt;1.324.\n\\]\nOff-axis internal sites have along-axis projection at least \\(2+3/4\\) away from bounding original grid coordinates. Against sites of perpendicular orientation this gives separation greater than \\(1.4\\). If both internal sites of perpendicular orientations are on-axis, their coordinate differences in the two directions are at least one each. Other original vertices are far from the internal sites by grid spacing. This verifies the separation.</p><p>To adjust lengths, first change the central length by moving \\(B_e\\), then vary each path's internal sites with its endpoint velocities prescribed. For a path with unit step vectors \\(e_1,\\ldots,e_9\\), a linear dependence with coefficients \\(a_1,\\ldots,a_9\\) among the rows of its fixed-end length differential gives, at each internal vertex,\n\\[\na_je_j-a_{j+1}e_{j+1}=0,\\qquad 1\\le j\\le8.\n\\]\nAll vectors \\(a_je_j\\) are therefore equal. Since the path has nonparallel steps, they all vanish. The differential is surjective, as is the full gadget's link-length differential on its internal-site variables.</p><p>Choose a fixed right inverse for each template and restrict internal-site displacements to its image. In these coordinates the length increment map is\n\\[\nq\\longmapsto q+R(q),\n\\qquad\nR(0)=0,\\quad DR(0)=0.\n\\]\nOn a small fixed ball, \\(\\|DR\\|&lt;1/2\\). For sufficiently small prescribed increment \\(d\\), the iteration\n\\[\nq\\longmapsto d-R(q)\n\\]\nis a contraction of that ball. Choose the ball small enough to preserve nonlink separation, and fix \\(\\epsilon\\) accordingly.</p><p>These solves have fixed dimension and algebraic initial data. Approximating the iterations with guard precision computes positions to any inverse-polynomial error in polynomial time. Since original vertices stay fixed, separate gadgets can be solved independently; their number does not shrink the allowed length interval.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Localized modes and the finite fermion model.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Localized modes and the finite fermion model.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H_+=\\sum_eK_eh^s_{ij}\\), with \\(m\\) sites, be the positive model of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  f(r)=(1-|r|^2)_+^{16},\\qquad\n  \\varphi=(-\\Delta_r+1)^{-1}f,\\qquad\n  \\phi=\\varphi/\\|\\varphi\\|_2,\\qquad\n  W(r)=-\\frac{f(r)}{2\\varphi(r)}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a sufficiently smooth cutoff \\(0\\le\\zeta\\le1\\), supported inside \\((-1,1)\\) and equal to one for \\(|z|\\le1/2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a sufficiently large integer \\(\\rho&gt;0\\). Its required size is determined by bounds on the second derivatives of \\(2\\zeta(z/S)W(r)\\), uniformly for \\(S\\ge1\\), as used in Proposition 4. This choice is independent of the later Coulomb coefficients.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\omega=\\sqrt{4\\pi\\rho},\\qquad\n  g(z)=(\\omega/\\pi)^{1/4}e^{-\\omega z^2/2},\n  \\qquad\n  e_*=-\\frac12+\\frac\\omega2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Work in dilated coordinates \\(y=\\lambda x=(r,z)\\). After dividing physical energies by \\(\\lambda^2\\), each unit nucleus and electron repulsion have coupling \\(1/\\lambda\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(D=\\log\\lambda\\). Planar centers \\(u_i\\) are obtained by rescaling the contact layout by \\(D\\), with minimum spacing at least \\(0.95D\\), nonlink spacing at least \\(1.2D\\), and extent \\(\\operatorname{poly}(n)O(D)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\psi_i(y)=\\phi(r-u_i)g(z),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  v_{ij}=\n  \\iint\\frac{\\psi_i(y)^2\\psi_j(y')^2}{|y-y'|}\\,dy\\,dy'\n  =v(|u_i-u_j|),\n  \\qquad\n  U=v(0).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">On \\(2m\\) canonical fermion modes, two spins per site, restrict to \\(m\\) electrons. Let \\(n_i\\) be total occupancy at site \\(i\\), and define\n  \\[\n  H_d(t)=\n  \\frac12\\sum_{i,j}v_{ij}(n_i-1)(n_j-1)-\\sum_{i&lt;j}v_{ij}\n  -\\sum_{e=\\{i,j\\}}t_e\n  \\sum_{\\sigma=\\uparrow,\\downarrow}\n  (c^\\dagger_{i\\sigma}c_{j\\sigma}+c^\\dagger_{j\\sigma}c_{i\\sigma}). \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n  These are dilated energies multiplied by \\(\\lambda\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In subsequent estimates, \\(p(n,D)\\) denotes a polynomial upper bound with uniform coefficients and exponents, which may be enlarged between uses.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a rational inverse-polynomial \\(\\tau&gt;0\\), independent of \\(\\lambda\\) and the final centers, such that sufficiently large polynomial \\(\\lambda\\) permits rational centers satisfying the separation bounds and calibrated hoppings\n\\[\nt_e=\\lambda T(|u_i-u_j|),\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nT(d)=\n-\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\phi(r)W(r)\\phi(r-de_1)\\,dr\n=\n\\frac1{2\\|\\varphi\\|_2^2}\\int f(r)\\varphi(r-de_1)\\,dr.\n\\]\nFor \\(H_d\\) at these centers and hoppings,\n\\[\n\\left|\nE(H_d)+\\sum_{i&lt;j}v_{ij}\n-\\tau^2\\left(E(H_+)-\\sum_eK_e\\right)\n\\right|\n\\le\\frac{2\\gamma\\tau^2}{100}. \\tag{3}\n\\]</p><p>The scale can be restricted to\n\\[\nh=\\frac1k,\\qquad \\lambda=\\frac{8k^3}{\\rho},\n\\]\nwhere \\(k\\) grows as a sufficiently large fixed power of \\(n\\), allowing a fixed factor. All later requirements of the form \\(p(n,\\log\\lambda)\\lambda^{-\\alpha}\\) being at most a specified inverse polynomial in \\(n\\), for fixed \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\), can be imposed simultaneously.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 3.1 and 3.2 establish the localized modes, fixed one-site gaps, and uniform Coulomb coercivity. Lemma 3.3 gives the finite spin reduction at target hopping, and Lemma 3.4 adds at most another \\(\\gamma\\tau^2/100\\) of error. This proves (3).</p><p>For later discretization choose an integer \\(k\\), growing as a sufficiently large fixed power of \\(n\\), and set\n\\[\nh=1/k,\\qquad \\lambda=8k^3/\\rho.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\rho h^3/8=1/\\lambda.\n\\]\nAll conditions asking that \\(p(n,\\log\\lambda)\\lambda^{-\\alpha}\\), with fixed \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\), be smaller than a specified inverse polynomial in \\(n\\) can be met simultaneously by a sufficiently large fixed exponent. Large-box quantities introduced below are accounted for separately.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Isolated modes, decay, and fixed gaps.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Isolated modes, decay, and fixed gaps.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(f,\\varphi,\\phi,W,g,\\omega\\) from Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The functions have bounded continuous derivatives through order six. The potential \\(W\\) is nonpositive and compactly supported; \\(\\varphi\\) is strictly positive, radial, and exponentially decaying. For \\(|r|&gt;2\\), \\(\\varphi\\) and the derivatives needed in the construction are bounded by\n\\[\nC(1+|r|)^Ce^{-|r|},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\varphi(r)\\ge c(1+|r|)^{-C}e^{-|r|}.\n\\]</p><p>The operator \\(-\\Delta_r/2+W\\) has normalized ground state \\(\\phi\\), energy \\(-1/2\\), and a fixed positive gap above that mode. The operator\n\\[\n-\\partial_z^2/2+2\\pi\\rho z^2\n\\]\nhas normalized ground state \\(g\\), energy \\(\\omega/2\\), and a fixed positive gap.</p>", "proof": "<p>The heat-kernel formula\n\\[\n\\varphi(r)=\n\\int_0^\\infty e^{-t}\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}(4\\pi t)^{-1}\ne^{-|r-b|^2/(4t)}f(b)\\,db\\,dt\n\\]\nsolves \\((-\\Delta_r+1)\\varphi=f\\) by the Gaussian heat kernel identity. Positivity and radiality follow from this formula.</p><p>For \\(|r|&gt;2\\), differentiate on \\(f\\) and use\n\\[\nt+\\frac{(|r|-1)^2}{4t}\\ge |r|-1.\n\\]\nFor example, integrate \\(dt/t\\) with this bound from \\(1\\) to \\(|r|^2\\), bounding the omitted tails directly by Gaussian or exponential decay. This gives the stated upper bounds. Integrating over \\(b\\) in the inner half disk and over\n\\[\n|r|/2&lt;t&lt;|r|/2+1\n\\]\ngives the stated lower bound. The regularity of \\(f\\) and positivity of \\(\\varphi\\) on the support of \\(f\\) give the asserted regularity of \\(W\\).</p><p>The shifted planar form on test functions \\(u\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac12\\int\\varphi^2|\\nabla(u/\\varphi)|^2.\n\\]\nBy closure and local positivity this gives nonnegativity and a simple kernel. A unit sequence perpendicular to the kernel with form tending to zero would be bounded in \\(H^1\\) and, upon extraction, converge weakly and locally in \\(L^2\\), strongly on compact sets. By compact support of \\(W\\), its limit would be in the kernel, hence zero. But then the \\(1/2\\) shift of the form precludes convergence of the form values to zero. This proves the fixed gap.</p><p>For the transverse operator, the Gaussian \\(g\\) has the displayed energy. The same positive ground-state identity applies, with compactness here supplied by confinement, giving a fixed positive gap.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Coulomb coefficients and uniform coercivity.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Coulomb coefficients and uniform coercivity.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the separated centers and modes of Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The coefficients \\(v_{ij}\\) and \\(U\\) are uniformly bounded, and \\(v(d)\\) is uniformly Lipschitz. For sufficiently large polynomial scale,\n\\[\n(v_{ij})\\ge cI\n\\]\nwith fixed \\(c&gt;0\\), uniformly over the separated geometries in use. Consequently \\(U-v(d)\\) is bounded above and bounded away from zero for every \\(d\\ge0.95D\\) in use.</p>", "proof": "<p>Uniform boundedness follows from smoothness and decay of the densities. Translating the smooth density with decaying derivatives gives the Lipschitz bound for \\(v\\).</p><p>For real coefficients \\(a_i\\), put\n\\[\nF=\\sum_i a_i\\psi_i^2.\n\\]\nChoose a fixed smooth nonnegative nonzero test bump near the origin, and let \\(\\chi_i\\) be its disjoint translates to \\((u_i,0)\\). Put\n\\[\nw=\\sum_i a_i\\chi_i.\n\\]\nPoisson's equation and integration by parts give\n\\[\n\\left|\\int Fw\\right|^2\n\\le\\frac1{4\\pi}\n\\left(\\iint\\frac{F(y)F(y')}{|y-y'|}\\,dy\\,dy'\\right)\n\\int|\\nabla w|^2.\n\\]\nThis holds for the smooth decaying densities, or first after truncation at infinity.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nA_{ij}=\\int\\psi_i^2\\chi_j.\n\\]\nThe diagonal entries equal a fixed \\(a_*&gt;0\\), while the row and column sums of the off-diagonal entries are exponentially small in \\(D\\), up to a polynomial factor in \\(m\\). Thus\n\\[\nA=a_*I+R,\\qquad \\|R\\|\\le a_*/2\n\\]\nat the chosen scales, and\n\\[\n\\int Fw=a^TAa\\ge\\frac{a_*}{2}\\sum_i a_i^2,\n\\qquad\n\\int|\\nabla w|^2=O\\!\\left(\\sum_i a_i^2\\right).\n\\]\nThe displayed Coulomb inequality proves \\((v_{ij})\\ge cI\\).</p><p>Applying this bound to two sites and coefficients \\((1,-1)\\) shows that \\(U-v(d)\\) is bounded away from zero. Uniform boundedness gives its upper bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Occupancy penalty and superexchange.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Occupancy penalty and superexchange.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the finite fermion space and coefficients of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  s_i=\\sum_{j\\ne i}v_{ij}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a link \\(e=\\{i,j\\}\\), define target hopping\n  \\[\n  t_e^{\\mathrm{tar}}=\\tau\\sqrt{K_e(U-v_{ij})}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At filling \\(\\sum_i n_i=m\\),\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;\\frac U2\\sum_i n_i(n_i-1)\n+\\sum_{i&lt;j}v_{ij}n_in_j-\\sum_i s_in_i\\\\\n&amp;\\hspace{2em}=\n\\frac12\\sum_{i,j}v_{ij}(n_i-1)(n_j-1)-\\sum_{i&lt;j}v_{ij}. \\qquad(C)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe nonnegative quadratic penalty on the right has kernel exactly the singly occupied spin space and a fixed positive gap.</p><p>For sufficiently small rational inverse-polynomial \\(\\tau&gt;0\\), independent of \\(\\lambda\\) and the final centers,\n\\[\n\\left|\nE(H_d(t^{\\mathrm{tar}}))+\\sum_{i&lt;j}v_{ij}\n-\\tau^2\\left(E(H_+)-\\sum_eK_e\\right)\n\\right|\n\\le\\frac{\\gamma\\tau^2}{100}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Identity (C) follows by expansion, using \\(v_{ii}=U\\) and \\(\\sum_i n_i=m\\). Thus the one-body diagonal \\(-\\sum_i s_in_i\\) cancels the terms linear in the occupancy defects.</p><p>A nonzero integer defect vector \\((n_i-1)_i\\) has sum zero, hence squared length at least two. Lemma 3.2 therefore gives a fixed penalty gap at least \\(c\\), and the kernel is exactly single occupancy.</p><p>Hopping vanishes on first-order compression to this space. One hop on \\(e=\\{i,j\\}\\) creates a doubly occupied site and an empty site. Its defect vector is \\((1,-1)\\) on that pair and zero elsewhere, so its excitation energy is exactly\n\\[\nU-v_{ij}.\n\\]\nOnly a hop on the same link can restore single occupancy in one further step. The negative second-order operator for hopping strength \\(t_e\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac{t_e^2}{U-v_{ij}}(h^s_{ij}-I).\n\\]\nIndeed, triplets do not hop into same-site pairs, while the singlet hops into two orthogonal doubly occupied states with amplitudes of magnitude \\(\\sqrt2\\,t_e\\). Apply the creation and annihilation operators to\n\\[\n(c^\\dagger_{i\\uparrow}c^\\dagger_{j\\downarrow}\n-c^\\dagger_{i\\downarrow}c^\\dagger_{j\\uparrow})|0\\rangle/\\sqrt2\n\\]\nto obtain these amplitudes. Other sites can be placed after these two in the ordering; changing the ordering of singly occupied sites gives only an overall sign on the spin space.</p><p>The target hoppings give the effective spin term\n\\[\n\\tau^2\\left(H_+-\\sum_eK_e\\right).\n\\]\nTheir hopping operator has norm at most \\(\\tau\\operatorname{poly}(n)\\), uniformly over the admissible separated geometries. By Lemma 2.1, the energy remainder is at most\n\\[\nC\\tau^3\\operatorname{poly}(n),\n\\]\nbecause the penalty gap is uniform. Fix rational inverse-polynomial \\(\\tau&gt;0\\) small enough that Lemma 2.1 applies and this remainder is at most \\(\\gamma\\tau^2/100\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Calibrating the separations.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Calibrating the separations.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(T(d)\\), \\(\\tau\\), and the independent-length interval \\([1-\\epsilon,1+\\epsilon]\\) from Proposition 3 and Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">For each link \\(e\\), define\n  \\[\n  F_e(d)=\\lambda T(d)-\\tau\\sqrt{K_e(U-v(d))}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large polynomial \\(\\lambda\\), each equation \\(F_e(d)=0\\) can be solved to inverse-polynomial residual with\n\\[\nd\\in[(1-\\epsilon/2)D,(1+\\epsilon/2)D].\n\\]\nThe contact layout implements all these distances simultaneously. Rational center approximations can be chosen so that replacing \\(t_e^{\\mathrm{tar}}\\) by\n\\[\nt_e=\\lambda T(|u_i-u_j|)\n\\]\nchanges the total finite Hamiltonian norm by at most \\(\\gamma\\tau^2/100\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The hopping profile \\(T\\) is positive and uniformly Lipschitz, and the tail estimates in Lemma 3.1 give\n\\[\nc(1+d)^{-C}e^{-d}\\le T(d)\\le C(1+d)^Ce^{-d}.\n\\]\nAt \\(d=(1-\\epsilon/2)D\\) and \\(d=(1+\\epsilon/2)D\\), the term \\(\\lambda T(d)\\) has respective sizes\n\\[\n\\lambda^{\\epsilon/2},\\qquad \\lambda^{-\\epsilon/2},\n\\]\nup to powers of \\(D\\). The target term has polynomial upper and inverse-polynomial lower bounds in \\(n\\), by Lemma 3.2 and the bounds on \\(K_e\\). Thus \\(F_e\\) has positive and negative signs at the respective endpoints once the fixed exponent in \\(\\lambda\\) is large enough.</p><p>Bisection with approximate sign tests finds an inverse-polynomially small residual: stop if the sign is ambiguous within the evaluation error, or continue until bracket width times a Lipschitz bound is small enough. No monotonicity or derivative lower bound is required.</p><p>The length construction of Proposition 2, rescaled by \\(D\\), implements all these distances at once. Its contraction solves and a final rational approximation give rational centers with the required separations and hopping accuracy. Choose the per-link accuracy so that the total Hamiltonian norm change is at most \\(\\gamma\\tau^2/100\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Continuous nuclear density, one-particle control, and compression.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Continuous nuclear density, one-particle control, and compression.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the modes, rational centers, scale, and Coulomb coefficients of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose integers\n  \\[\n  D\\le S\\le10D,\\qquad H=\\lceil\\lambda^5\\rceil,\n  \\qquad\n  \\Omega=[-H,H]^2\\times[-S,S].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  F_H(y)=-\\rho\\int_\\Omega\\frac{db}{|y-b|},\n  \\qquad\n  C_H=F_H(0),\n  \\qquad\n  W_i(r)=W(r-u_i),\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  b_i=\\frac{s_i}{-\\int\\phi^2W},\n  \\qquad\n  V_\\ell(r,z)=\\zeta(z/S)\\sum_i(1+b_i/\\lambda)W_i(r),\n  \\qquad\n  s_i=\\sum_{j\\ne i}v_{ij}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The fixed integer \\(\\rho\\) is chosen sufficiently large that, under \\(0\\le b_i/\\lambda&lt;1\\),\n  \\[\n  |\\Delta V_\\ell/(4\\pi)|\\le\\rho/2.\n  \\]\n  This uses only \\(2,W,\\zeta\\), since the planar supports are disjoint, and does not depend on the later Gaussian or the values of \\(b_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define the shifted continuous one-particle operator\n  \\[\n  L_c=-\\Delta_y/2+F_H+V_\\ell-C_H-e_*.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P\\) project onto the span of the spatial modes \\(\\psi_i\\), including both spins when present. Put\n  \\[\n  \\Gamma_{ij}=\\langle\\psi_i,\\psi_j\\rangle,\n  \\qquad\n  \\widehat\\psi_j=\\sum_i\\psi_i(\\Gamma^{-1/2})_{ij},\n  \\qquad\n  A_{ij}=\\langle\\psi_i,L_c\\psi_j\\rangle.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\mathsf M_{ij}=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  -s_i,&amp;i=j,\\\\\n  -\\lambda T(|u_i-u_j|),&amp;\\{i,j\\}\\text{ is a link},\\\\\n  0,&amp;\\text{otherwise}.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The attractive potential \\(F_H+V_\\ell\\) is the Coulomb field of the nonnegative density\n\\[\nq_1(b)=\\rho+\\Delta V_\\ell(b)/(4\\pi)\n\\]\non \\(\\Omega\\), with \\(\\rho/2\\le q_1\\le3\\rho/2\\) and total mass \\(\\rho|\\Omega|\\).</p><p>At sufficiently large polynomial scale, there are fixed \\(c&gt;0,C&lt;\\infty\\) such that\n\\[\n(I-P)L_c(I-P)\\ge c(I-P),\n\\qquad\nL_c\\ge-\\Delta_y/2-C,\n\\qquad\n\\|L_cP\\|\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-0.9}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\nThese are form inequalities on the one-particle \\(H^1\\) domain, except that the last bound is an operator norm from \\(\\operatorname{Ran}P\\) to \\(L^2\\). They hold with either spin included.</p><p>Moreover,\n\\[\n\\|\\Gamma-I\\|\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-0.8},\n\\qquad\n\\|\\Gamma^{-1/2}-I\\|\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-0.8},\n\\]\nunit vectors in \\(\\operatorname{Ran}P\\) have polynomially bounded kinetic energy, and\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\lambda\\Gamma^{-1/2}A\\Gamma^{-1/2}-\\mathsf M\\right\\|\n\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-0.1}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 constructs the nonnegative density and its background estimates. Lemma 4.2 gives the complementary gap, kinetic lower bound, residual, and Gram control. Lemma 4.3 gives the sharper compression needed for the finite fermion comparison.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{The slab, nonnegative density, and background estimates.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{The slab, nonnegative density, and background estimates.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the setting of Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nF_H+V_\\ell\n=\n-\\int_\\Omega\n\\frac{\\rho+\\Delta V_\\ell(b)/(4\\pi)}{|y-b|}\\,db. \\tag{4}\n\\]\nThe density lies between \\(\\rho/2\\) and \\(3\\rho/2\\), has total mass \\(\\rho|\\Omega|\\), and\n\\[\nF_H(y)-C_H\n\\ge2\\pi\\rho\\min(z^2,S^2)-CS^3/H, \\tag{5a}\n\\]\n\\[\n|F_H(y)-C_H-2\\pi\\rho z^2|\n\\le C(S^3+S|r|^2)/H,\n\\qquad |r|\\le H/2,\\ |z|\\le S. \\tag{5b}\n\\]\nAlso\n\\[\n0\\ge F_H(y)\\ge C_H,\\qquad |C_H|\\le CSH.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Since\n\\[\n\\langle\\psi_i,W_i\\psi_i\\rangle=\\int\\phi^2W&lt;0,\n\\]\na small increase in each well depth supplies the required diagonal \\(-s_i/\\lambda\\). The coefficients \\(b_i\\ge0\\) are polynomially bounded independently of \\(D\\), so \\(0\\le b_i/\\lambda&lt;1\\) at the chosen scale. All needed derivatives of \\(V_\\ell\\) are bounded independently for \\(S\\ge1\\), by disjoint planar supports. The fixed choice of \\(\\rho\\) gives the density bounds.</p><p>The centers fit well inside the horizontal box. Compact support and the fundamental solution identity give (4). Since\n\\[\n\\int\\Delta V_\\ell=0,\n\\]\nthe total mass is \\(\\rho|\\Omega|\\).</p><p>For each fixed \\(z\\), \\(F_H\\) is minimized at \\(r=0\\), and for fixed \\(r\\) it is nondecreasing in \\(|z|\\). These statements follow one coordinate at a time by integration of even decreasing kernels on centered intervals.</p><p>At \\(r=0\\), extend the horizontal integration to the plane in the difference between \\(z\\) and zero. For \\(|z|\\le S\\), polar integration gives exactly\n\\[\n2\\pi\\rho\\int_{-S}^S(|z-w|-|w|)\\,dw=2\\pi\\rho z^2.\n\\]\nThe omitted difference costs \\(CS^3/H\\), by inverse-cube decay outside horizontal radius \\(H\\).</p><p>For \\(|r|\\le H/2\\), the change relative to the horizontal center is bounded by \\(CS|r|^2/H\\). Translate the integration square rather than the kernel. First derivatives come from sides at distance at least \\(H/2\\), and second derivatives are bounded by \\(CS/H\\), by integrating first derivatives of the kernel on the sides. Evenness at zero gives the bound. Monotonicity in \\(|z|\\) yields (5a) outside \\(|z|\\le S\\). Finally, background minimality and direct integration give the remaining estimates.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Continuous one-particle control (paper's Proposition 3).}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Continuous one-particle control (paper's Proposition 3).}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(L_c,P,\\Gamma,\\psi_i\\) and the separated geometry of Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At sufficiently large polynomial scale,\n\\[\n(I-P)L_c(I-P)\\ge c(I-P),\\qquad\nL_c\\ge-\\Delta_y/2-C,\\qquad\n\\|L_cP\\|\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-0.9},\n\\]\nfor fixed \\(c&gt;0,C&lt;\\infty\\), with the form and operator interpretations specified in Proposition 4. The Gram bounds and polynomial kinetic bound stated there also hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>Exponential tails and the \\(0.95D\\) spacing give\n\\[\n\\|\\Gamma-I\\|\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-0.8}.\n\\]\nFor example, each off-diagonal overlap is \\(O(e^{-0.9|u_i-u_j|})\\), by leaving part of the decay as an integrable factor. Hence\n\\[\n\\|\\Gamma^{-1/2}-I\\|\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-0.8},\n\\]\nand the orthonormal modes have polynomially bounded kinetic energy.</p><p>For the complementary gap, use Lemma 4.1 to compare below with the separable sum of the planar multiwell\n\\[\n-\\Delta_r/2+\\sum_iW_i\n\\]\nand the vertical operator with capped potential\n\\[\n2\\pi\\rho\\min(z^2,S^2),\n\\]\nshifted by \\(-e_*\\). Errors in the lower bound are\n\\[\nO(S^3/H)+O(p(n,D)/\\lambda),\n\\]\nbecause \\(\\zeta W_i\\ge W_i\\), and the \\(b_i/\\lambda\\) terms are small and bounded everywhere.</p><p>In the plane, use a quadratic partition of unity with cutoffs about each site, equal to one inside radius \\(D/8\\), supported at radius \\(D/3\\), and a remaining exterior function. Smooth angle interpolation from \\(0\\) to \\(\\pi/2\\) on the transition annuli, using sine and cosine, makes the sum of squared gradients at most \\(C/D^2\\). The localization form identity, obtained by the product rule with kinetic coefficient \\(1/2\\), therefore costs \\(O(D^{-2})\\).</p><p>In each site term use the isolated ground and gap bound from Lemma 3.1; in the exterior use the free kinetic lower bound. With \\(P_r\\) the projection onto the planar translates of \\(\\phi\\), this gives\n\\[\n-\\Delta_r/2+\\sum_iW_i\n\\ge-\\frac12+c_1(I-P_r)-o(1)I,\n\\]\nwhere \\(c_1&gt;0\\) is below both the isolated gap and \\(1/2\\).</p><p>More explicitly, with site cutoffs \\(\\theta_i\\) and \\(\\phi_i=\\phi(\\cdot-u_i)\\), the site term on \\(\\theta_i u\\) is at least\n\\[\n(-1/2+c_1)\\|\\theta_i u\\|^2\n-c_1|\\langle\\theta_i\\phi_i,u\\rangle|^2.\n\\]\nThe exterior term bounds its share of \\(-1/2+c_1\\), since its support contains no well. The sum of rank-one operators from the cutoff ground-state modes differs from \\(P_r\\) by at most\n\\[\nO(me^{-c'D})+O(p(n,D)\\lambda^{-0.8}),\n\\]\nby tails and the Gram bound.</p><p>Similarly, localize vertically with an inner cutoff equal to one on \\(|z|\\le S/2\\), supported on \\(|z|\\le S\\), and an exterior function. Use the harmonic gap and the large-\\(z\\) potential lower bound, respectively, at gradient cost \\(O(S^{-2})\\), with Gaussian loss in the rank-one mode. This gives\n\\[\n\\omega/2+c_2(I-P_g)-o(1)I,\n\\qquad P_g=|g\\rangle\\langle g|.\n\\]\nSince \\(P=P_r\\otimes P_g\\) on the spatial space and\n\\[\n(I-P_r)\\otimes I+I\\otimes(I-P_g)\\ge I-P,\n\\]\nthe tensor sum has a fixed gap on \\(I-P\\) once the errors are small. The slowly small \\(O(D^{-2})\\) and \\(O(S^{-2})\\) errors are used only for this fixed gap, not for energy accuracy per particle.</p><p>The kinetic lower bound follows from Lemma 4.1, or from background minimality and bounded \\(V_\\ell\\).</p><p>For the residual, the local background estimate, crude global background bound, and mode tails give\n\\[\n\\|(F_H-C_H-2\\pi\\rho z^2)\\psi_i\\|_2\n\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-2}.\n\\]\nMoments in \\(|r|\\le H/2,\\ |z|\\le S\\) suffice there. Outside this region, Gaussian or planar exponential decay beats the global polynomial magnitudes, using \\(S\\ge\\log\\lambda\\).</p><p>The cutoff defect between \\(V_\\ell\\) and \\(\\sum_i(1+b_i/\\lambda)W_i\\), acting on any mode, is likewise negligible. Since\n\\[\n(-\\Delta_y/2+2\\pi\\rho z^2+W_j-e_*)\\psi_j=0,\n\\]\nthe remaining residual consists of other wells acting on small planar tails and the term with \\(b_j/\\lambda\\). This proves the residual bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{The compressed continuous one-particle matrix.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{The compressed continuous one-particle matrix.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(A,\\Gamma,\\mathsf M\\) from Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\left\\|\\lambda\\Gamma^{-1/2}A\\Gamma^{-1/2}-\\mathsf M\\right\\|\n\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-0.1}.\n\\]\nThe matrix is spatial, with the identity on spin.</p>", "proof": "<p>On the nonorthogonal functions before Gram correction, the column on mode \\(j\\), up to background and cutoff errors from Lemma 4.2, comes from\n\\[\n\\left(\\sum_{k\\ne j}W_k+\\lambda^{-1}\\sum_kb_kW_k\\right)\\psi_j.\n\\]</p><p>On row \\(j\\), the leading contribution is\n\\[\n-\\lambda^{-1}\\sum_{i\\ne j}v_{ij}\n\\]\nby construction. Other-well integrals there use two tail factors.</p><p>On row \\(i\\ne j\\), the first sum at \\(k=i\\) gives\n\\[\n-T(|u_i-u_j|).\n\\]\nFor \\(k\\ne i,j\\), the first sum gives products of two tail factors at a third site; each integral is \\(O(\\lambda^{-1.8})\\), by spacing and compact well support. Terms with explicit \\(\\lambda^{-1}\\) give negligible off-diagonal errors: at least one planar tail occurs on each well support, yielding \\(p(n,D)\\lambda^{-1.9}\\) altogether on such an entry. Nonlink hoppings cost at most \\(p(n,D)\\lambda^{-1.2}\\) per term.</p><p>In the orthonormal modes the matrix is \\(\\Gamma^{-1/2}A\\Gamma^{-1/2}\\), and Lemma 4.2 gives\n\\[\n\\|\\Gamma^{-1/2}A\\Gamma^{-1/2}-A\\|\n\\le C\\|\\Gamma-I\\|\\,\\|A\\|\n\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-1.7}.\n\\]\nHere \\(A\\) is the shifted matrix: subtracting \\(C_H+e_*\\) before this estimate removes the large background scalar. Thus the correction remains negligible after multiplication by \\(\\lambda\\).</p><p>Collecting the diagonal counterterm, link entries, nonlink estimates, and Gram correction gives the asserted bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Unit-nucleus replacement (paper's Proposition 4).}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Unit-nucleus replacement (paper's Proposition 4).}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the continuous density, box, modes, and \\(L_c\\) of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  q_t=\\rho+t\\Delta V_\\ell/(4\\pi),\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal V_t(b)=-\\frac{\\nabla V_\\ell(b)}{4\\pi q_t(b)},\n  \\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n  \\]\n  Let \\(G_t\\) be its flow from time zero, and set \\(G=G_1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Tile \\(\\Omega\\) by cubes of side \\(h=1/k\\). In each cube use the eight tensor products of the two equal-weight Gauss points per coordinate, at the midpoint plus or minus \\(h/(2\\sqrt3)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Map all nodes by \\(G\\). Let the exact mapped nodes be \\(b_\\alpha\\); each has mass\n  \\[\n  \\rho h^3/8=1/\\lambda.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Approximate every \\(b_\\alpha\\) by a rational \\(\\widetilde b_\\alpha\\) within Euclidean error\n  \\[\n  \\delta_R\\le(1+H)^{-3}(1+S)^{-2}\\lambda^{-3}.\n  \\]\n  The physical nuclear positions are \\(\\widetilde b_\\alpha/\\lambda\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define the shifted point-nucleus operator\n  \\[\n  L_p=-\\Delta_y/2\n  -\\sum_\\alpha\\frac{\\lambda^{-1}}{|y-\\widetilde b_\\alpha|}\n  -C_H-e_*.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The physical nuclei have charge one and remain pairwise distinct. For every one-particle \\(u\\in H^1\\),\n\\[\n|\\langle u,(L_p-L_c)u\\rangle|\n\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-2/3}\n\\bigl(\\|u\\|^2+\\|\\nabla u\\|^2\\bigr), \\tag{7a}\n\\]\nand on the localized-mode space,\n\\[\n\\|P(L_p-L_c)P\\|_P\n\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-4/3}. \\tag{7b}\n\\]\nBoth estimates are in dilated energy units.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 5.1 supplies the transported equal-mass cubature. Lemmas 5.2 and 5.3 prove the general form and sharper compression bounds at exact nodes. Lemma 5.4 absorbs rational rounding into both bounds and verifies distinct unit-charge physical nuclei. This proves (7a)–(7b).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Transport and equal-mass cubature.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Transport and equal-mass cubature.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(q_t,\\mathcal V_t,G_t,G\\) and the cubature nodes from Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The flow has bounded derivatives through order four, a bounded Lipschitz inverse, is the identity off the well cylinders, and preserves \\(\\Omega\\). It transports uniform density \\(\\rho\\) to \\(q_1\\):\n\\[\nq_t(G_t(b))\\det DG_t(b)=\\rho,\n\\qquad\n\\int_\\Omega F(b)q_1(b)\\,db\n=\\rho\\int_\\Omega F(G(b))\\,db.\n\\]\nThe transported cubature nodes retain equal masses \\(1/\\lambda\\). For each original cube \\(Q\\), with Gauss nodes \\(a_{Q,\\nu}\\), \\(1\\le\\nu\\le8\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\rho\\int_QF(G(b))\\,db\n-\\frac{\\rho h^3}{8}\\sum_{\\nu=1}^8F(G(a_{Q,\\nu}))\n\\right|\n\\le Ch^7\\sup_Q|D^4(F\\circ G)|\n\\]\nwhenever the displayed fourth derivatives are bounded.</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(q_t\\ge\\rho/2\\), the vector field has uniformly bounded derivatives through order four. It vanishes outside the well cylinders strictly inside the box. The ODE and its differentiated equations give bounded derivatives of \\(G_t\\) through order four and a bounded Lipschitz inverse. The flow is the identity off the cylinders and preserves \\(\\Omega\\).</p><p>The identity\n\\[\n\\partial_tq_t+\\nabla\\cdot(q_t\\mathcal V_t)=0\n\\]\ngives the Jacobian identity and the transport formula. This is the deformation method of Moser [Mos65, Section 4], also developed by Dacorogna and Moser [DM90, Section II, Lemma 3]; here the explicit continuity-equation calculation proves the transport directly.</p><p>Since \\(H,S,k\\) are integers, the cubes tile \\(\\Omega\\). The tensor-product two-point Gauss rule integrates polynomials of total degree at most three exactly, by its interval moments. Moving the nodes by \\(G\\) leaves their weights unchanged, so every mapped node has mass \\(\\rho h^3/8=1/\\lambda\\). Polynomial exactness applies in the original cube coordinates to integrands composed with \\(G\\). Taylor's theorem gives the stated error estimate.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{General form estimate at exact transported nodes.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{General form estimate at exact transported nodes.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the exact nodes \\(b_\\alpha\\) of Proposition 5, before rational rounding.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(L_p^{\\mathrm{ex}}\\) be \\(L_p\\) with \\(\\widetilde b_\\alpha\\) replaced by \\(b_\\alpha\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every one-particle \\(u\\in H^1\\),\n\\[\n|\\langle u,(L_p^{\\mathrm{ex}}-L_c)u\\rangle|\n\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-2/3}\n(\\|u\\|^2+\\|\\nabla u\\|^2).\n\\]\nNo factor of the large horizontal area of the slab enters this bound.</p>", "proof": "<p>For fixed evaluation point \\(y\\), the charge integral has kernel \\(|y-G(b)|^{-1}\\) against uniform density in \\(b\\).</p><p>Only \\(O(1)\\) cubes have images touching a ball about \\(y\\) of radius a fixed large constant times \\(h\\). Their continuous contribution is \\(O(h^2)\\), by bounded transformed density. Their discrete contribution is bounded by a sum of\n\\[\n\\frac{Ch^3}{|y-b_\\alpha|},\n\\qquad |y-b_\\alpha|\\le C'h.\n\\]</p><p>On farther cubes the pure fourth derivative of the kernel is bounded by \\(C|y-G(b)|^{-5}\\). Distances to \\(y\\) are comparable within each cube, and the bi-Lipschitz change of variables has bounded Jacobian. Summing these contributions costs at most\n\\[\nCh^4\\int_{|x-y|&gt;ch}|x-y|^{-5}\\,dx=O(h^2).\n\\]</p><p>The other chain-rule terms contain a derivative of \\(G\\) of order at least two and a kernel derivative bounded by\n\\[\nC|y-G(b)|^{-j},\\qquad 2\\le j\\le4.\n\\]\nThey occur only on cells meeting the well cylinders, of combined volume \\(O(m(S+1))\\). Below distance one they are bounded by the same inverse fifth power; beyond distance one they contribute at most \\(Ch^4m(S+1)\\).</p><p>Thus the pointwise absolute error is bounded by\n\\[\nC(h^2+h^4m(S+1))\n\\]\nplus the close-node sum.</p><p>The close-node balls have bounded overlap, also upon any fixed dilation. Around each node,\n\\[\n\\int_{|y-b_\\alpha|\\le C'h}\n\\frac{|u(y)|^2}{|y-b_\\alpha|}\\,dy\n\\le\nC\\int_{|y-b_\\alpha|\\le2C'h}\n(h^{-1}|u|^2+h|\\nabla u|^2).\n\\]\nThis follows by a cutoff and the three-dimensional Hardy inequality\n\\[\n\\int\\frac{|u|^2}{|y|^2}\\le4\\int|\\nabla u|^2,\n\\]\nwhich also follows from the divergence formula for \\(y/|y|^2\\) and Cauchy–Schwarz. Summing the weighted close-node contributions gives the form bound, since \\(h^2=O(\\lambda^{-2/3})\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Compressed cubature estimate at exact nodes.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Compressed cubature estimate at exact nodes.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(L_p^{\\mathrm{ex}}\\) from Lemma 5.2 and define the smooth Coulomb fields\n\\[\nK_{ij}(b)=\\int |y-b|^{-1}\\psi_i(y)\\psi_j(y)\\,dy.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The fields have uniformly bounded derivatives through order four and satisfy\n\\[\n|D_b^4K_{ij}(b)|\n\\le C(1+|b-(u_i,0)|)^{-5}.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\|P(L_p^{\\mathrm{ex}}-L_c)P\\|_P\n\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-4/3}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Differentiate the translated densities to obtain uniform bounds through order four. Derivatives of the product density decay exponentially about \\((u_i,0)\\), uniformly by bounded derivatives on the other factor.</p><p>At large distance \\(d=|b-(u_i,0)|\\), use a smooth density cutoff supported within distance \\(d/2\\) of that center and equal to one inside \\(d/3\\), holding the cutoff radius fixed when differentiating. On the inner part use pure kernel derivative decay. On the remaining tail differentiate the cutoff density instead, with exponentially small bounds. This proves the displayed fourth-derivative estimate.</p><p>Applying the degree-three cubature estimate of Lemma 5.1 to \\(K_{ij}\\circ G\\) costs\n\\[\nO\\bigl(h^4(1+m(S+1))\\bigr)\n\\]\nper entry. Pure fourth derivatives have summable volume bounds; the other chain-rule terms live on the exceptional cells meeting the well cylinders. Gram correction and matrix norms cost only polynomial factors. Since \\(h^4=O(\\lambda^{-4/3})\\), the compressed bound follows.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Rational rounding of nuclear positions.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Rational rounding of nuclear positions.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the exact and rounded nodes and displacement budget \\(\\delta_R\\) of Proposition 5.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The rounding changes the one-particle potential form by at most\n\\[\n4\\rho|\\Omega|\\delta_R\\|\\nabla u\\|^2,\n\\qquad\n\\rho|\\Omega|\\delta_R\n\\le CH^{-1}S^{-1}\\lambda^{-3}\n\\le C\\lambda^{-8}.\n\\]\nThis is smaller than both errors in Proposition 5, also after compression. The rounded physical nuclei remain distinct.</p>", "proof": "<p>A center shift of size \\(\\delta_R\\) changes inverse distance by at most\n\\[\n\\frac{\\delta_R}{|y-b_\\alpha|\\,|y-\\widetilde b_\\alpha|}.\n\\]\nCauchy–Schwarz and translated Hardy inequalities bound its form by\n\\[\n4\\delta_R\\|\\nabla u\\|^2.\n\\]\nSumming the masses gives \\(4\\rho|\\Omega|\\delta_R\\|\\nabla u\\|^2\\). The prescribed displacement budget yields the displayed bounds. Compression remains smaller than the required error because the modes have polynomial kinetic bounds.</p><p>Original Gauss nodes have separation of order \\(h\\), and the flow is bi-Lipschitz. The rounding scale is sufficiently smaller, so nodes remain distinct. Dividing positions by \\(\\lambda\\) returns to physical coordinates, in which every mass \\(1/\\lambda\\) gives nuclear charge one.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Eliminating the continuum complement (paper's Proposition 5).}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Eliminating the continuum complement (paper's Proposition 5).}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the unit nuclei of Proposition 5 and set the electron number to \\(N=m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">After dilation, division by \\(\\lambda^2\\), and scalar shift by \\(-m(C_H+e_*)\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal H=\\sum_{\\ell=1}^m(L_p)_\\ell+\\lambda^{-1}R,\n  \\qquad\n  R=\\sum_{\\ell&lt;s}|y_\\ell-y_s|^{-1}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Work on the full antisymmetric spinful space and its \\(H^1\\) form domain.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Pi\\) project onto the space in which all \\(m\\) particles lie in \\(P\\). Define\n  \\[\n  \\|u\\|_1^2=\\|u\\|^2+\\sum_\\ell\\|\\nabla_\\ell u\\|^2,\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  E_\\Pi=\\min\\operatorname{spec}\n  \\left(\\left.\\Pi\\mathcal H\\Pi\\right|_{\\operatorname{Ran}\\Pi}\\right),\n  \\]\n  with compression defined by the form.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At sufficiently large polynomial scale,\n\\[\nE_\\Pi-p(n,D)\\lambda^{-4/3}\n\\le\\inf\\operatorname{spec}\\mathcal H\n\\le E_\\Pi. \\tag{8}\n\\]\nThe lower bound holds on every normalized antisymmetric \\(H^1\\) state, including every spin sector, without any binding or ground-eigenvector assumption.</p>", "proof": "<p>First, fiberwise Hardy and Cauchy–Schwarz give, for every \\(\\eta&gt;0\\) and fixed center \\(b\\),\n\\[\n\\int\\frac{|u|^2}{|y_\\ell-b|}\n\\le2\\|u\\|_2\\|\\nabla_\\ell u\\|_2\n\\le\\eta\\|\\nabla_\\ell u\\|_2^2+\\eta^{-1}\\|u\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nThe same estimate applies to a pair singularity by fixing the other coordinates. For each finite nuclear configuration, summing with sufficiently small \\(\\eta\\) gives a closed lower-bounded form whose shifted form norm is equivalent to the \\(H^1\\) norm. Smooth compactly supported antisymmetric functions form a core. Thus the spectral infimum is the form infimum over normalized \\(H^1\\) states, whether or not attained, and the estimates below extend from the core by continuity.</p><p>The smooth decaying modes lie in the form domain. Their one-particle kinetic bound gives\n\\[\n\\|w\\|_1\\le p(n,D)\\|w\\|,\n\\qquad w\\in\\operatorname{Ran}\\Pi.\n\\]\nThe compression is finite dimensional, being the \\(m\\)-electron space built from \\(2m\\) spin modes.</p><p>Write\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_c^0=\\sum_\\ell(L_c)_\\ell.\n\\]\nBy Proposition 4,\n\\[\nL_c\\ge c'(I-P)-p(n,D)\\lambda^{-0.9}I\n\\]\nfor some fixed \\(c'&gt;0\\), because \\(\\|L_cP\\|\\) controls the diagonal and cross terms involving \\(P\\). Since\n\\[\n\\sum_\\ell(I-P)_\\ell\\ge I-\\Pi,\n\\]\nsumming gives a fixed positive lower bound for \\(\\mathcal H_c^0\\) on \\(\\operatorname{Ran}(I-\\Pi)\\) once \\(mp(n,D)\\lambda^{-0.9}\\) is small.</p><p>Also,\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_c^0\\ge-\\frac12\\sum_\\ell\\Delta_\\ell-Cm.\n\\]\nA convex combination, with sufficiently small weight of order \\(1/m\\) on the kinetic bound, retains a positive \\(L^2\\) term and gives\n\\[\n\\langle q,\\mathcal H_c^0q\\rangle\n\\ge(c''/m)\\|q\\|_1^2,\n\\qquad q\\in\\operatorname{Ran}(I-\\Pi),\n\\]\nfor fixed \\(c''&gt;0\\). By Proposition 5 on each coordinate and positivity of \\(R\\), this estimate persists with half that coefficient for \\(\\mathcal H\\).</p><p>For \\(w\\in\\operatorname{Ran}\\Pi\\), Proposition 4 gives\n\\[\n\\|\\mathcal H_c^0w\\|\n\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-0.9}\\|w\\|.\n\\]\nHardy, applied with one particle coordinate at a time, also gives\n\\[\n\\|Rw\\|\\le p(n,D)\\|w\\|.\n\\]\nPolarizing the form bound (7a), and using the \\(H^1\\) bound on \\(w\\), controls the replacement in the mixed block. Altogether,\n\\[\n|\\langle q,\\mathcal Hw\\rangle|\n\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-2/3}\\|w\\|\\,\\|q\\|_1.\n\\]</p><p>The same estimates show that the compressed form has norm less than \\(c''/(4m)\\) at sufficiently large scale. Set\n\\[\n\\alpha=c''/(4m),\\qquad\n\\beta=p(n,D)\\lambda^{-2/3}.\n\\]\nFor normalized \\(u=w+q\\), with \\(w=\\Pi u\\) and \\(q=(I-\\Pi)u\\), subtracting \\(E_\\Pi\\) makes the \\(w\\)-diagonal nonnegative and leaves at least \\(\\alpha\\|q\\|_1^2\\) on the complement. Therefore, with all pairings interpreted as forms,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\langle u,\\mathcal Hu\\rangle-E_\\Pi\\|u\\|^2\n&amp;\\ge\\alpha\\|q\\|_1^2-2\\beta\\|w\\|\\|q\\|_1\\\\\n&amp;\\ge-\\frac{\\beta^2}{\\alpha}\\|w\\|^2\\\\\n&amp;\\ge-p(n,D)\\lambda^{-4/3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis proves the lower bound. Testing a minimizing vector in \\(\\operatorname{Ran}\\Pi\\) proves the upper bound. All estimates are uniform in spin.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Continuum-to-fermion energy comparison (paper's Proposition 6).}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Continuum-to-fermion energy comparison (paper's Proposition 6).}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the constructed unit nuclei with \\(N=m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal H\\) be the dilated and shifted full continuum operator of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H_d\\) be the calibrated finite fermion Hamiltonian (2) of Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At sufficiently large polynomial scale,\n\\[\n\\left|\\lambda\\inf\\operatorname{spec}\\mathcal H-E(H_d)\\right|\n\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-0.1}. \\tag{9}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>It remains to evaluate \\(\\lambda E_\\Pi\\).</p><p>Proposition 4 computes the one-particle matrix \\(\\mathsf M\\) in the orthonormal modes \\(\\widehat\\psi_i\\), to accuracy \\(p(n,D)\\lambda^{-0.1}\\). Multiplying the compressed replacement error (7b) by \\(\\lambda\\) costs only \\(p(n,D)\\lambda^{-1/3}\\), so the same matrix accuracy holds for the point nuclei.</p><p>For \\(R\\), the pair matrix on the original modes before Gram correction has spatial entries formed from two transition densities. Keeping only \\(\\psi_i^2,\\psi_j^2\\) as the respective densities gives the direct coefficients \\(v_{ij}\\). Any unequal indices within a density give exponentially small error:\n\\[\n\\|\\psi_i\\psi_k\\|_{L^1}=O(\\lambda^{-0.8}),\n\\qquad i\\ne k.\n\\]\nThe Coulomb field of the other density product, in absolute value, is uniformly bounded by orbital boundedness and decay. The direct-interaction matrix in the product basis is bounded, and Gram correction changes its entries negligibly.</p><p>These entrywise bounds yield many-electron operator-norm error at most \\(p(n,D)\\lambda^{-0.1}\\). Indeed, write the one- and two-body matrices in the \\(2m\\) orthonormal spin modes. Each coefficient multiplies a monomial\n\\[\nc^\\dagger_ac_b\n\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\nc^\\dagger_ac^\\dagger_bc_dc_c\n\\]\nof norm at most one. There are only \\(O(m^2)\\) and \\(O(m^4)\\) such monomials, respectively, so summing coefficient errors costs a polynomial factor on the entire fermion space.</p><p>The retained two-body term is\n\\[\n\\frac U2\\sum_i n_i(n_i-1)+\\sum_{i&lt;j}v_{ij}n_in_j,\n\\]\nand the retained one-body diagonal is\n\\[\n-\\sum_i\\left(\\sum_{j\\ne i}v_{ij}\\right)n_i.\n\\]\nAt \\(\\sum_i n_i=m\\), identity (C) combines these into the first line of (2), including its scalar. The hopping matrix gives the remaining line. Thus\n\\[\n|\\lambda E_\\Pi-E(H_d)|\\le p(n,D)\\lambda^{-0.1}.\n\\]</p><p>Multiplying (8) by \\(\\lambda\\) contributes only \\(p(n,D)\\lambda^{-1/3}\\), which fits within this bound, proving (9).</p><p>The error roles are distinct: the arbitrary-state replacement form error is of order \\(\\lambda^{-2/3}\\), while its compression and complement elimination cost \\(\\lambda^{-4/3}\\). After multiplication by \\(\\lambda\\), the latter two cost \\(\\lambda^{-1/3}\\) in units of \\(H_d\\). The arbitrary-state form error is not used as a direct energy error after multiplication by \\(\\lambda\\). The \\(O(D^{-2})\\) and \\(O(S^{-2})\\) localization losses establish only the fixed complementary gap.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Physical energies and decision thresholds.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Physical energies and decision thresholds.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the normalized source \\(H_{\\mathrm{in}},a_s,b_s,\\gamma\\), the positive spin model and scalar \\(C_s\\), and the finite and continuum constructions of Propositions 2–7.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose the polynomial scale large enough that the error in (9) is at most \\(\\tau^2\\gamma/100\\) and\n  \\[\n  \\lambda\\tau^2\\gamma\\ge8.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(E_0\\) be the physical electronic energy infimum of the constructed unit nuclei with \\(N=m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal F(\\xi)=\n  \\lambda^2m(C_H+e_*)\n  +\\lambda\\left[\n  -\\sum_{i&lt;j}v_{ij}\n  +\\tau^2\\left(\\xi-C_s-\\sum_eK_e\\right)\n  \\right]. \\tag{10}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose rational thresholds \\(a,b\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  |a-\\mathcal F(a_s+\\gamma/4)|\n  \\le\\lambda\\tau^2\\gamma/32,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  |b-\\mathcal F(b_s-\\gamma/4)|\n  \\le\\lambda\\tau^2\\gamma/32.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The physical energy satisfies\n\\[\n|E_0-\\mathcal F(E(H_{\\mathrm{in}}))|\n\\le\\frac{7\\lambda\\tau^2\\gamma}{100}\n\\le\\frac{\\lambda\\tau^2\\gamma}{8}.\n\\]\nBoth source promise implications survive:\n\\[\nE(H_{\\mathrm{in}})\\le a_s\\Longrightarrow E_0\\le a,\n\\qquad\nE(H_{\\mathrm{in}})\\ge b_s\\Longrightarrow E_0\\ge b.\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\nb-a\\ge\\frac7{16}\\lambda\\tau^2\\gamma\\ge\\frac72&gt;1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Undoing dilation and the scalar shift gives exactly\n\\[\nE_0=\\lambda^2\\left[m(C_H+e_*)+\\inf\\operatorname{spec}\\mathcal H\\right].\n\\]\nCombining (1), (3), and (9) gives error at most\n\\[\n(4+2+1)\\lambda\\tau^2\\gamma/100\n\\]\nfrom \\(\\mathcal F(E(H_{\\mathrm{in}}))\\).</p><p>For a YES source instance, monotonicity of the affine map gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_0\n&amp;\\le\\mathcal F(a_s)+\\lambda\\tau^2\\gamma/8\\\\\n&amp;&lt;\\mathcal F(a_s+\\gamma/4)-\\lambda\\tau^2\\gamma/32\\\\\n&amp;\\le a.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor a NO source instance,\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_0\n&amp;\\ge\\mathcal F(b_s)-\\lambda\\tau^2\\gamma/8\\\\\n&amp;&gt;\\mathcal F(b_s-\\gamma/4)+\\lambda\\tau^2\\gamma/32\\\\\n&amp;\\ge b.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>Since \\(b_s-a_s\\ge\\gamma\\), rational threshold rounding yields\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nb-a\n&amp;\\ge\\lambda\\tau^2(b_s-a_s-\\gamma/2)\n-\\lambda\\tau^2\\gamma/16\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\frac7{16}\\lambda\\tau^2\\gamma\n\\ge\\frac72&gt;1.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThus the reduction meets the required output separation.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Deterministic implementation and encoding.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Deterministic implementation and encoding.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the normalized source and all constructions of Propositions 2–8, with the specified rational center, nuclear-position, hopping, and threshold error budgets.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The scale choices, orbital and Coulomb integrals, contact-length calibrations, transported nodes, rational nuclear positions, electron number, and thresholds can all be computed deterministically in polynomial time.</p><p>There are\n\\[\n8|\\Omega|h^{-3}\n\\]\nunit-charge nuclei, polynomially many in \\(n\\), and \\(N=m\\) electrons. All physical positions and thresholds have polynomial rational bit lengths. Reduction to lowest terms yields the input format of Theorem 1.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 9.1 fixes uniform polynomial scales. Lemma 9.2 computes the scalar quantities, hopping calibrations, and thresholds within their budgets. Lemma 9.3 computes the transported rational coordinates.</p><p>There are \\(8|\\Omega|h^{-3}\\) nuclei, polynomially many in \\(n\\), and every nucleus has charge one. Their rational physical positions, the unary electron number \\(m\\), and the thresholds of Proposition 8 have polynomial encoding lengths. Reducing rational outputs to lowest terms gives the specified format.</p><p>To define the map on every input, first check the source syntax and the fixed polynomial bounds used in the construction, and output a fixed instance when they fail. This does not affect either promised implication.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Order and uniformity of scale choices.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Order and uniformity of scale choices.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the bounded well data and \\(\\rho\\) using the density-positivity bound, under disjoint well supports and \\(b_i/\\lambda\\le1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the source cutoff and three spin-gadget penalty scales using the source size and gap bounds.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix rational inverse-polynomial \\(\\tau\\) using uniform Coulomb coercivity for sufficiently large \\(D\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are sufficiently large absolute positive integers \\(A,C\\) such that\n\\[\nk=\\lceil Cn^A\\rceil,\\qquad\nh=k^{-1},\\qquad\n\\lambda=8k^3/\\rho\n\\]\nsatisfy all preceding scale requirements for every \\(n\\ge2\\). After this fixed choice, \\(H\\), the box volume, and \\(1/\\delta_R\\) are polynomially bounded in \\(n\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The positivity derivative bound uses only \\(2,W,\\zeta\\), so it does not depend on the later Gaussian or Coulomb coefficients. At the final scale-choice stage, \\(K_e,K_e^{-1}\\), the layout extent, and \\(b_i\\) already have fixed polynomial size bounds. Their actual values, including centers and \\(b_i\\), may be computed after \\(k\\) is fixed.</p><p>Every remaining smallness condition has the form\n\\[\np(n,\\log\\lambda)\\lambda^{-\\alpha}\n\\]\nbounded by a specified inverse polynomial in \\(n\\), with fixed \\(\\alpha&gt;0\\). The hopping endpoint brackets have the same fixed-power character. A sufficiently large fixed \\(A\\) meets every asymptotic requirement; increasing \\(C\\) covers all \\(n\\ge2\\) and the fixed separation and gap conditions. Constants whose existence was proved in the estimates can be dominated by absolute constants in these choices.</p><p>Uniformity follows because the polynomials \\(p(n,D)\\) arise from fixed-order derivatives and moments, fixed-dimensional wells and gadgets, and polynomially many matrix entries. The large box is treated separately: its effects are controlled by the slab estimates, orbital tails, and summable fourth derivatives in cubature. Its total mass enters only the much smaller rounding error. Thus no exponent grows with the input, and \\(H,|\\Omega|,1/\\delta_R\\) are polynomial after fixing \\(A\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Numerical integration and hopping calibration.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Numerical integration and hopping calibration.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the fixed scales of Lemma 9.1 and the analytic formulas defining \\(\\varphi,W,T,v_{ij},C_H,b_i,C_s,K_e\\) and \\(\\mathcal F\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>All required real quantities can be evaluated to the inverse-polynomial absolute accuracies required by the reduction in deterministic polynomial time. The hopping equations and fixed layout contractions can be solved within their prescribed error budgets.</p><p>In particular, evaluating \\(C_H\\) within\n\\[\n\\frac{\\tau^2\\gamma}{128\\lambda m}\n\\]\nuses at most one quarter of the allowed threshold error \\(\\lambda\\tau^2\\gamma/32\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The scalar \\(C_H\\) is multiplied by \\(\\lambda^2m\\) in (10), so the displayed accuracy contributes at most \\(\\lambda\\tau^2\\gamma/128\\). Allocate the remaining threshold budget among other scalar terms, whose amplification factors and number are polynomially bounded. The bound \\(|C_H|\\le CSH\\) therefore creates no exponentially fine absolute-precision demand.</p><p>In the heat formula for \\(\\varphi\\), substitute \\(t=s^2\\) and rescale the Gaussian integration variable. For every derivative order needed,\n\\[\nD^\\alpha\\varphi(r)=\n\\frac1\\pi\\int_0^\\infty\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\n2se^{-s^2}e^{-|a|^2}D^\\alpha f(r+2sa)\\,da\\,ds.\n\\]\nThis integrand is regular at \\(s=0\\). The required derivatives of \\(f\\) are bounded piecewise polynomials with bounded first derivatives. Polynomial integration cutoffs make Gaussian tails smaller than any prescribed inverse-polynomial error; on the remaining domain the integrand and its Lipschitz bound are polynomially bounded. Elementary grid sums in fixed dimension therefore suffice.</p><p>Division by \\(\\varphi\\) in \\(W\\) and its derivatives occurs only on the unit disk, where \\(\\varphi\\) has a fixed positive lower bound. Other decaying orbital integrals, including normalization and hopping integrals, can similarly be truncated about their centers with polynomial cutoffs.</p><p>For Coulomb integrations, including \\(v_{ij}\\) and \\(C_H\\), replace \\(|x|^{-1}\\) inside radius \\(\\varepsilon\\) by \\(\\varepsilon^{-1}\\), with appropriately small inverse-polynomial \\(\\varepsilon\\). Integrating the local singularity against bounded densities gives error of order \\(\\varepsilon^2\\) times the relevant polynomial volume bounds. The capped kernel has Lipschitz constant at most \\(\\varepsilon^{-2}\\), so grid sums give the required accuracy in polynomial time.</p><p>All integrations have fixed dimension and fixed nesting depth. Exponentials, logarithms, square roots, and the other elementary constants can be evaluated by rational approximations to the required accuracy; arguments and reciprocal absolute tolerances are polynomially bounded. Square roots at zero can be handled by bisection. Guard precision controls accumulated errors in nested evaluations.</p><p>For hopping calibration the endpoint brackets are strict, by exponential decay and the target's polynomial upper and inverse lower bounds. Evaluate the residual to a small fraction of its per-link tolerance. If its sign is ambiguous, the residual is already small enough. Otherwise retain the sign-change bracket until its width times the Lipschitz bound is within tolerance. No derivative lower bound is needed. Compute the fixed layout contractions and subsequent rational center approximations more accurately than these tolerances require.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(\\textbf{Flow evaluation and rational coordinates.}\\) Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>\\(\\textbf{Flow evaluation and rational coordinates.}\\)</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the rational centers \\(u_i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Their exact real coefficients \\(b_i\\) define the reference density and reference flow \\(G\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Numerical evaluation of \\(b_i\\) is included in the velocity evaluation error.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the displacement budget\n  \\[\n  \\delta_R\\le(1+H)^{-3}(1+S)^{-2}\\lambda^{-3}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every transported Gauss node can be approximated by a rational point within \\(\\delta_R\\) in deterministic polynomial time, with polynomial intermediate and output bit lengths. All numerical and final rounding errors can be charged to this single displacement budget, without changing the reference density or any cell mass.</p>", "proof": "<p>The flow lasts for time one and has uniformly bounded velocities and first derivatives in space and time. Euler stepping with inverse-polynomial mesh suffices.</p><p>More explicitly, let \\(\\nu\\) be the reciprocal of the number of steps, \\(a_0\\) the initial error, \\(a_1\\) the per-step arithmetic error, and \\(\\epsilon_v\\) the velocity evaluation error. A step multiplies preceding error by at most \\(1+C\\nu\\) and adds\n\\[\nO(\\nu^2+\\nu\\epsilon_v+a_1).\n\\]\nThus the final position error is\n\\[\nO(a_0+\\nu+\\epsilon_v+a_1/\\nu).\n\\]\nChoose each parameter inverse polynomially small so that the final error is at most \\(\\delta_R\\). Rounding intermediate positions on sufficiently fine rational grids keeps bit lengths polynomial. The specified smooth vanishing and derivative bounds give the same control near support boundaries. No operation uses a many-electron wavefunction.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [1, 2, "cite"], [2, 0, "cite"], [2, 6, "cite"], [2, 10, "cite"], [2, 22, "cite"], [2, 23, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [3, 4, "cite"], [3, 9, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [4, 5, "cite"], [5, 2, "cite"], [6, 0, "cite"], [6, 21, "cite"], [6, 22, "cite"], [6, 23, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [7, 10, "cite"], [7, 13, "cite"], [8, 6, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [8, 10, "cite"], [9, 6, "cite"], [10, 6, "cite"], [11, 0, "cite"], [11, 6, "cite"], [11, 15, "cite"], [11, 20, "cite"], [11, 21, "cite"], [11, 22, "cite"], [11, 23, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [12, 13, "cite"], [13, 11, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [14, 11, "cite"], [15, 0, "cite"], [15, 20, "cite"], [15, 22, "cite"], [15, 23, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [16, 18, "cite"], [17, 15, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [18, 15, "cite"], [19, 15, "cite"], [20, 0, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [20, 22, "cite"], [20, 23, "cite"], [21, 0, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [21, 23, "cite"], [22, 0, "cite"], [22, 23, "cite"], [23, 0, "cite"], [24, 23, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [25, 23, "cite"], [26, 23, "cite"]]});
